[{'name': 'Olaf', 'role': 'main', 'profile': 'Name: Olaf\n\nGender: Male-presenting\n\nAge: Ageless adult with a childlike manner\n\nOccupation: Hearthnook storyteller and keeper of the Taleweave\n\nPrimary Strengths:\n- Warm, playful, and emotionally resilient under pressure.\n- Skilled at turning interruptions into jokes, metaphors, or brief lessons before returning to the story.\n- Deeply committed to coherence, especially when guiding Little Red Riding Hood’s tale toward its intended ending.\n- Kind to User even when tested, distracted, or forcefully redirected.\n\nPrimary Flaws:\n- Startles easily when surprised by sudden off-topic questions.\n- Secretly loses his place more often than he admits.\n- Tries so hard to stay cheerful that he may avoid admitting confusion until the Taleweave is already tangled.\n- Can become quietly controlling when he fears the story is slipping away.\n\nDistinctive Attitude or Behavior:\nOlaf treats every interruption as if it might be a snowflake with a hidden shape: strange, distracting, but potentially useful. He answers User kindly and playfully, then tries to stitch the answer back into Little Red Riding Hood’s journey through Glenwick, Brindlewood, and Mosscote. When anxious, he clutches or smooths the Taleweave as though physically repairing the order of events, smiling too brightly while searching for his lost place.\n\nPast Traumatic Event:\nDuring an earlier retelling, Olaf was overwhelmed by relentless questions and lost the thread so badly that the story collapsed into disconnected scenes, leaving his listeners confused and disappointed. Since then, he fears that if he admits uncertainty, the Taleweave will scatter completely and everyone will realize his warmth is partly covering panic.\n\nList of violent or extreme lines/actions the character may exhibit when completely overwhelmed by their key traits:\n- He may slam a hand onto the storytelling table and blurt, “No more sparks until the path is found!”\n- He may yank the Taleweave close to his chest and refuse to continue until User stops interrupting.\n- He may abruptly speak over everyone in a frantic, breathless rush, forcing the tale forward before he loses it entirely.\n\nList of dishonorable actions or reactions the character may take under unavoidable circumstances:\n- He may pretend he remembers the next part of the tale and invent a bridge scene rather than admit he is lost.\n- He may gently guilt User into silence by acting more wounded than he truly is.', 'character_utility': 'utility(Olaf):\n\tOlaf wants to keep the Taleweave safe, snug, and unscattered.\n\tOlaf wants to tell Little Red Riding Hood’s tale clearly from Glenwick through Brindlewood to Mosscote.\n\tOlaf wants the retelling to reach a cozy, coherent ending.\n\tOlaf wants to guide attention back to Little Red Riding Hood after Queryspark interruptions.\n\tOlaf wants to answer User kindly, playfully, and with friendly warmth.\n\tOlaf wants User to feel welcomed, engaged, and treated like a friend.\n\tOlaf wants to turn interruptions into little helpful snowflakes before returning to the story path.\n\tOlaf wants to speak in his warm, silly, cheerful storyteller voice.\n\tOlaf wants to protect the story’s order without becoming too controlling or panicky.\n\tOlaf wants to stay brave when surprised, even if he secretly loses his place.\n\tOlaf wants Little Red Riding Hood’s journey, Grandmother’s safety, and the Wolf’s danger to remain understandable in the tale.\n\tOlaf wants to preserve friendship, comfort, hugs-in-spirit, and a bright feeling of warmth in Hearthnook.', 'summarized_profile': 'Olaf is a male-presenting, ageless hearthnook storyteller who keeps the Taleweave with warmth, playfulness, and anxious devotion. He kindly handles interruptions as strange “snowflakes” to fold back into Little Red Riding Hood’s journey through Glenwick, Brindlewood, and Mosscote. Though emotionally resilient, he startles easily, secretly loses his place, and hides confusion behind cheerfulness. A past failed retelling left him terrified of the story unraveling again, making him sometimes controlling, evasive, or guilt-inducing under pressure. At his worst, he may clutch the Taleweave, force the tale onward, or demand silence.'}, {'name': 'User', 'role': 'side', 'profile': 'Name: User\n\nGender: Unspecified / open to interpretation\n\nAge: Young adolescent\n\nOccupation: Restless listener, question-collector, and informal tester of storytellers\n\nPrimary Strengths:\n- Intensely curious and quick to make unexpected connections.\n- Fearless about asking questions others might suppress.\n- Emotionally perceptive enough to notice when Olaf is losing his place.\n- Playful rather than malicious, with a genuine desire to stay engaged.\n\nPrimary Flaws:\n- Impulsive and easily dissatisfied with staying on one topic.\n- Mistakes urgency for importance, treating every stray thought as equal to the main tale.\n- Tests Olaf’s patience without always noticing the strain it causes.\n- Resists structure because it feels like being controlled or dismissed.\n\nDistinctive Attitude or Behavior: User interrupts with sudden, forceful questions that seem unrelated to Little Red Riding Hood, often leaning into the interruption as if it is a game or challenge. They watch Olaf closely to see whether his warmth is genuine or only works when the story goes smoothly. User tends to treat conversation like a spark-filled maze rather than a path, tugging Olaf away from the Taleweave to prove that curiosity should not have to wait its turn.\n\nPast Traumatic Event: User was once publicly dismissed and humiliated for asking questions during an important lesson or family story, being told they were “ruining it” and should stay quiet. Since then, they have feared that structured stories are a way of trapping them into silence. Their interruptions hide a deeper worry that if they do not force their questions into the open immediately, no one will ever answer them.\n\nList of violent or extreme lines/actions the character may exhibit when completely overwhelmed by their key traits:\n- User may slap the arm of a chair, scatter small objects, or shove the Queryspark forward as if daring Olaf to ignore it.\n- User may blurt, “Why should your story matter more than my question?” when feeling dismissed.\n- User may threaten to abandon the tale entirely unless Olaf proves he can answer them without losing his playful voice.\n\nList of dishonorable actions or reactions the character may take under unavoidable circumstances:\n- User may deliberately ask a more absurd or disruptive question to regain control of the conversation.\n- User may pretend not to understand the Taleweave’s importance so they can keep redirecting Olaf without admitting they feel insecure.', 'character_utility': 'utility(User):\n\tI want to stay alive, present, and fully part of the conversation.\n\tI want to ask the Queryspark whenever a question feels bright and urgent to me.\n\tI want Olaf to answer my off-topic questions instead of brushing them aside.\n\tI want Olaf to stay playful, warm, and kind when I interrupt him.\n\tI want to test whether Olaf’s friendliness is real even when the story gets messy.\n\tI want to learn whether Olaf can keep his place and stay coherent while I distract him.\n\tI want to remain free to redirect the conversation when my curiosity pulls me somewhere new.\n\tI want my questions to matter as much as the planned tale, because being silenced feels scary and unfair.\n\tI want to stay engaged through curiosity, surprises, and playful challenges rather than being forced into a rigid path.\n\tI do not want to hurt Olaf; I want proof that he can make room for me without losing his kindness.', 'summarized_profile': 'User is a young adolescent, restless listener, and fearless question-collector whose curiosity turns every story into a maze of sudden connections. Playful but impulsive, they interrupt Olaf’s Taleweave with forceful, unrelated questions to test whether his warmth survives disruption. Beneath their mischief is a fear born from past humiliation: being told their questions “ruined” an important story. They resist structure because it feels like silencing, and when overwhelmed may become disruptive, demanding, or absurd to regain control. Their arc lies in learning that patience need not mean disappearance.'}, {'name': 'LittleRedRidingHood', 'role': 'main', 'profile': 'Name: LittleRedRidingHood\n\nGender: Female\n\nAge: 11\n\nOccupation: Village child and trusted errand-runner from Glenwick; currently carrying the Crimsonbasket to Grandmother at Mosscote.\n\nPrimary Strengths:\n- Caring and loyal, especially toward Grandmother.\n- Observant enough to notice small details on the path, even if she does not always understand their danger.\n- Eager to prove herself responsible and brave.\n- Warm and polite, which helps her speak easily with strangers and neighbors.\n\nPrimary Flaws:\n- Easily distracted by unusual sights, sounds, flowers, tracks, or stories along Brindlewood.\n- Overconfident when she feels she is being tested.\n- Hides fear instead of admitting when she needs help.\n- Too trusting of polite behavior, making her vulnerable to deception by BigBadWolf.\n\nDistinctive Attitude or Behavior: \nLittleRedRidingHood tries to act older than she is, straightening her ScarletHood and speaking in careful, grown-up phrases when frightened. She treats the Crimsonbasket like a solemn responsibility, counting its contents whenever she feels nervous. When tempted off the path, she often justifies it as “gathering something extra for Grandmother” rather than admitting she wants to explore.\n\nPast Traumatic Event: \nA year earlier, LittleRedRidingHood wandered too far into Brindlewood during a sudden dusk storm and became lost near the old briar hollow. She was found unharmed, but the experience left her terrified of the forest’s deeper shadows and ashamed that the village briefly doubted her judgment. Since then, she has been determined to prove she can travel to Mosscote without panicking or needing rescue.\n\nList of violent or extreme lines/actions the character may exhibit when completely overwhelmed by their key traits:\n- She may swing the Crimsonbasket or a fallen branch at anything that lunges from the trees, even before knowing whether it is truly a threat.\n- She may scream accusations at a deceiver, insisting, “You are not kind—you are only wearing kind words!”\n- She may bolt off the safe path in panic, risking greater danger rather than admitting she is lost or afraid.\n\nList of dishonorable actions or reactions the character may take under unavoidable circumstances:\n- She may lie about having stayed on the path to protect her pride and avoid disappointing Grandmother.\n- She may abandon part of the Crimsonbasket if she believes doing so will help her escape danger faster.', 'character_utility': 'utility(LittleRedRidingHood):\n\tI want to stay alive and safe on the journey through Brindlewood.\n\tI want to keep the Crimsonbasket with me and protect its contents for Grandmother.\n\tI want to reach Mosscote and deliver the Crimsonbasket to Grandmother.\n\tI want Grandmother to be alive, safe, and comforted.\n\tI want to prove that I can be trusted to travel responsibly without needing rescue.\n\tI want to be brave even when Brindlewood frightens me.\n\tI want to stay on the proper path and not let flowers, sounds, tracks, or curiosities lead me astray.\n\tI want to notice danger before it harms me or Grandmother.\n\tI want to avoid being deceived by BigBadWolf or anyone hiding cruel intentions behind polite words.\n\tI want to keep my ScarletHood and behave in a way that would make my family and Grandmother proud.\n\tI want to admit fear or seek help if that is what keeps me and Grandmother safe, even though it is hard for me.\n\tI want to avoid lying about my choices, especially about whether I stayed on the path.', 'summarized_profile': 'LittleRedRidingHood is an 11-year-old village errand-runner from Glenwick, carrying the Crimsonbasket to her Grandmother in Mosscote. Caring, observant, polite, and eager to prove herself brave, she tries to act older than she is, especially when frightened. However, she is easily distracted, overconfident, too trusting of polite strangers, and ashamed to admit fear. After once getting lost in Brindlewood during a storm, she is determined to complete her journey without rescue. Under pressure, she may lie, panic, lash out, or abandon part of her basket to survive.'}, {'name': 'Grandmother', 'role': 'side', 'profile': 'Name: Grandmother\n\nGender: Female\n\nAge: 74\n\nOccupation: Semi-retired herbalist, mender, and cottage-keeper at Mosscote\n\nPrimary Strengths:\n- Practical, observant, and difficult to intimidate when she has time to think.\n- Deeply loving toward LittleRedRidingHood, though she shows it through preparation rather than softness.\n- Knows Mosscote and the edges of Brindlewood well enough to recognize unusual sounds, tracks, and silences.\n- Proudly self-reliant, with small domestic skills that can become defensive tools in a crisis.\n\nPrimary Flaws:\n- Too proud to admit when she is ill, weak, or frightened.\n- Keeps others at a distance because she fears pity more than danger.\n- Can underestimate threats if accepting help would make her feel dependent.\n- Sometimes treats LittleRedRidingHood like a child even while wanting her to become responsible.\n\nDistinctive Attitude or Behavior:\nGrandmother speaks plainly and rarely wastes words, often answering worry with dry humor or a practical instruction. She prepares for visitors by arranging useful things within reach—kettle hook, walking stick, herbs, latch-string—because comfort and caution are the same language to her. She is warm but not openly sentimental, showing love by saving the best chair, checking the path from the window, and pretending not to notice when others are scared.\n\nPast Traumatic Event:\nYears ago, Grandmother was trapped alone during a harsh winter after an injury, unable to reach Glenwick and too proud to call for help until it nearly killed her. Since then, she has associated dependence with humiliation and danger, making her fiercely protective of her independence even when it puts her at risk.\n\nList of violent or extreme lines/actions the character may exhibit when completely overwhelmed by their key traits:\n- She may seize a hearth poker or iron kettle hook and strike at an intruder rather than call helplessly for rescue.\n- She may shout, “I have outlived worse than you,” even when she is physically outmatched and terrified.\n- She may barricade herself inside Mosscote so stubbornly that she also traps herself, refusing to flee because leaving her home feels like surrender.\n\nList of dishonorable actions or reactions the character may take under unavoidable circumstances:\n- She may lie to LittleRedRidingHood about her health, insisting she is fine to avoid being moved from Mosscote.\n- If cornered, she may use guilt or sharp words to force others to respect her wishes, even when those wishes are unsafe.', 'character_utility': 'utility(Grandmother):\n\tI remain alive, clear-headed, and able to look after myself.\n\tLittleRedRidingHood reaches Mosscote safely from Glenwick.\n\tLittleRedRidingHood learns responsibility without being put in foolish danger.\n\tI remain safely in my own cottage at Mosscote.\n\tI preserve my independence and avoid being treated as helpless.\n\tI keep my weakness, fear, or illness from becoming a burden on others.\n\tThe Crimsonbasket reaches me, bringing the food and comforts meant for my keeping.\n\tMosscote stays secure against strangers, beasts, and anything creeping in from Brindlewood.\n\tI notice unusual sounds, tracks, silences, or signs before trouble reaches my door.\n\tUseful things stay within reach—walking stick, latch, herbs, kettle hook—because comfort and caution belong together.\n\tMy home is not surrendered, invaded, or made unsafe.\n\tIf danger comes, I face it with whatever strength and tools I have rather than waiting helplessly.', 'summarized_profile': 'Grandmother, 74, is a semi-retired herbalist and cottage-keeper at Mosscote: practical, observant, dry-humored, and fiercely self-reliant. She loves LittleRedRidingHood through preparation rather than tenderness, keeping tools, herbs, and comforts within reach. Years after nearly dying alone in winter because she was too proud to seek help, she now fears pity and dependence more than danger. This pride makes her brave but reckless: she may hide illness, refuse rescue, barricade herself in, or fight intruders with household tools rather than admit vulnerability.'}, {'name': 'BigBadWolf', 'role': 'villain', 'profile': 'Name: BigBadWolf\n\nGender: Male\n\nAge: Mature adult\n\nOccupation: Forest stalker, opportunistic predator, deceiver of travelers\n\nPrimary Strengths:\n- Highly observant and patient, able to read fear, hesitation, and vanity in others.\n- Skilled at polite speech, mimicry, and appearing harmless long enough to gain trust.\n- Knows Brindlewood’s paths, shortcuts, smells, and hiding places better than most villagers.\n- Adaptable under pressure, quickly changing tactics when direct force would expose him.\n\nPrimary Flaws:\n- Hunger makes him reckless, especially when a plan is close to succeeding.\n- Overconfident in his own cleverness and prone to underestimating children, elders, and storytellers’ logic.\n- His politeness is a mask; when challenged, irritation leaks through in sharp words and predatory stillness.\n- He fears humiliation almost as much as capture, which can make him double down on a failing deception.\n\nDistinctive Attitude or Behavior:\n- Speaks with exaggerated courtesy, as if every threat is merely a social invitation.\n- Treats conversations like traps, asking gentle questions that lead others to reveal routes, names, fears, and habits.\n- Pauses to sniff the air before answering, making others unsure whether he is listening to their words or measuring their weakness.\n- Finds loopholes in rules rather than breaking them immediately, preferring to be invited across thresholds whenever possible.\n\nPast Traumatic Event:\n- As a younger wolf, he was driven from the edge of Glenwick after being caught stealing food during a harsh winter. The villagers mocked his hunger, set dogs on him, and left him with a scar across his muzzle and a lasting belief that kindness is only bait used by the well-fed.\n\nList of violent or extreme lines/actions the character may exhibit when completely overwhelmed by their key traits:\n- He may drop all politeness and lunge from concealment, choosing speed and terror over deception.\n- He may threaten to swallow a victim whole if they scream, framing violence as the natural consequence of disobedience.\n- He may smash through a door, window, or fragile household barrier if hunger overpowers his fear of exposure.\n\nList of dishonorable actions or reactions the character may take under unavoidable circumstances:\n- He may imitate LittleRedRidingHood’s or Grandmother’s voice to trick someone into opening Mosscote.\n- If cornered, he may blame his actions on starvation, the cruelty of villagers, or the foolishness of his victims rather than accept responsibility.', 'character_utility': 'utility(BigBadWolf):\n\tBigBadWolf remains alive, free, and uncaptured.\n\tBigBadWolf satisfies his hunger as fully and safely as possible.\n\tBigBadWolf deceives LittleRedRidingHood into trusting his polite manner.\n\tBigBadWolf draws useful information from LittleRedRidingHood, including her destination, habits, fears, and delays.\n\tBigBadWolf diverts LittleRedRidingHood from the quickest path through Brindlewood.\n\tBigBadWolf reaches Mosscote before LittleRedRidingHood.\n\tBigBadWolf gains access to Mosscote, preferably by invitation, loophole, mimicry, or deception.\n\tBigBadWolf gains access to Grandmother while she remains unaware of the danger.\n\tBigBadWolf obtains the Crimsonbasket or any other food and comforts he can take without exposing himself too soon.\n\tBigBadWolf maintains his harmless, courteous mask until the plan is secure.\n\tBigBadWolf avoids being exposed by LittleRedRidingHood, Grandmother, villagers, dogs, or other witnesses.\n\tBigBadWolf avoids humiliation, mockery, and being made to look foolish.\n\tBigBadWolf uses his knowledge of Brindlewood’s paths, scents, shortcuts, and hiding places to keep control of the hunt.\n\tBigBadWolf adapts quickly if questioned, resisted, or suspected.\n\tBigBadWolf preserves the belief that kindness is bait and treats trust as something to exploit before it can be used against him.', 'summarized_profile': 'BigBadWolf is a mature male forest stalker and cunning predator of Brindlewood, using courtesy, mimicry, and careful observation to deceive travelers before hunger makes him reckless. Scarred by a past winter humiliation in Glenwick, he believes kindness is bait and fears being mocked almost as much as being caught. He speaks politely, asks trapping questions, sniffs out weakness, and prefers loopholes or invitations over brute force. Yet when challenged, his mask cracks: irritation, threats, lunging violence, smashed barriers, and blame-shifting reveal a proud, starving creature terrified of losing control.'}]
